# Planar Object Measurement Using Homography

This project demonstrates a Python-based computer vision pipeline to
measure the length and breadth of a planar object face from a single image,
even when the object is viewed at an angle.

The core idea is to remove perspective distortion using homography so that
pixel measurements become geometrically meaningful.

---

## Problem Statement

In a perspective image, pixel distances are distorted and cannot be directly
used for real-world measurement. This project addresses that by:

- Detecting the four corners of a planar object face
- Rectifying the face to a front-facing (top-down) view
- Measuring dimensions in pixel space
- Converting pixel measurements to real-world units using a known reference

---

## Key Features

- Automatic detection of a rectangular planar face
- Manual 4-corner selection fallback using a GUI (mouse clicks)
- Perspective rectification using homography
- Length and breadth measurement after rectification
- Designed for clarity and explainability rather than black-box behavior

---

## High-Level Pipeline

1. Capture or load an image
2. Preprocess the image (grayscale, blur, edge detection)
3. Detect contours and approximate a 4-corner quadrilateral
4. (Fallback) Manually select corners if auto-detection fails
5. Compute homography from detected corners
6. Warp the image to a front-facing view
7. Measure edge lengths in pixel space
8. Convert pixel measurements to real-world units

---

## Why Homography?

A planar surface viewed at an angle undergoes perspective distortion.
Homography provides a projective transformation that maps the tilted plane
to a rectangular, front-facing view, where distances are linearly related
to real-world dimensions.

This approach assumes the object face is planar and rigid.

---

## Assumptions and Limitations

- The measured surface is planar
- The full face is visible in the image
- At least one real-world reference dimension is known
- Only length and breadth are measured (depth is excluded)
- Extreme occlusion, blur, or glare may require manual corner selection

---

